<!--
    GH-312: the positive-evidence-only tally for the discovered-no-return
    consumer (`option noreturn_discstrict on|off`, default-ON, DIV-92).

    `noreturn_disc` concludes a callee never returns once at least 3 of its call
    sites "have no valid fall-through". One of the three arms implementing that
    test is `!is_instruction_start(fall)` - and the Listing walk pushes every
    call's fall-through onto its worklist unconditionally, so a call's successor
    is ALWAYS attempted. That arm therefore fires exactly when kuna's own decoder
    failed at the successor, which is a fact about kuna, not about the callee.
    Three undecodable bytes are enough to forge the verdict for a function whose
    body is `mov $7,%eax ; ret`, and the engine then DELETES the live tail of
    every caller. `noreturn_discstrict` keeps the terminal arm (a call with no
    fall-through at all) and the two POSITIVE arms (the successor is data / is
    another function's entry) and drops the bare one.

    Like the whole no-return family (`noreturn_disc` / `noreturn_propagate` /
    `noreturn_error`, docs/history/listing-tier-design.md), this reads the
    program-wide Listing, which is built ONLY on the real-ELF bootstrap path and
    never on this XML `<binaryimage>` datatest path. So on the XML path the flag
    is INERT (there is no Listing to consume), and a two-pass off=bug/on=fix
    datatest is not constructible here. The end-to-end behavior - three
    undecodable bytes no longer deleting a caller's `return retseven() + 3` - is
    proven by the cross-crate gate
    `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_noreturn_discstrict.rs` against a
    real x86-64 fixture (`noreturn_discstrict_x86_64`), the same split
    `noreturn_error` uses (ghangr-noreturn-error.xml + verify_noreturn_error.rs).

    This datatest proves the P0 assertion is WIRED:
      * `option noreturn_discstrict off` PARSES and round-trips (an unknown option
        would make the console error and the second decompile would differ / not
        run), and
      * flipping it changes NOTHING about the rendered C on the XML path
        (default-inert parity): the same output appears under the default
        pipeline AND with `noreturn_discstrict off`.

    Reuses the GH-558 bytes as a small, deterministic loadable function.

    Source:           int gh558(int x) { if (x LE 8) return 1; return 0; }
